('node-6-36', 'phase-06-lesson-36-component-design-systems-headless-ui-arch', 'module-8', 36, 'Lesson 8.36: Component Design Systems & Headless UI Architecture', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 6.4, Lesson 6.11', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 6.4, Lesson 6.11 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 220, 7, 1500.0, 1800.0, '# Lesson 6.36: Component Design Systems & Headless UI Architecture

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 6.4, Lesson 6.11
- **Subtopics**:
  - `6.36.1` Design system architecture: design tokens, semantic naming (color, spacing, typography), and dark mode theming
  - `6.36.2` Headless UI pattern: separating component accessibility, keyboard navigation, and state logic from visual styling
  - `6.36.3` Radix UI / Floating UI primitives: anchor positioning, collision detection, portal rendering, and focus containment
  - `6.36.4` Tailwind CSS integration vs CSS Modules: class variance authority (cva), clsx, and tailwind-merge utility patterns
  - `6.36.5` Component polymorphism: the `asChild` pattern and polymorphic `as` prop implementations in TypeScript
  - `6.36.6` Building compound components: Select, DropdownMenu, Tabs, and Accordion with context communication
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Class name conflicts overriding critical design tokens; headless portal components breaking keyboard navigation order.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Build a production-ready, accessible, theme-aware DropdownMenu component using Radix primitives and Tailwind styling.
- **Project Application**: CompKit: Core design system component catalog.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 6 // Lesson 6.36: Component Design Systems & Headless UI Architecture\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Build a production-ready, accessible, theme-aware DropdownMenu component using Radix primitives and \n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "6.36", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Build a production-ready, accessible, theme-aware DropdownMenu component using Radix primitives and Tailwind styling.", "subtopics": ["6.36.1 Design system architecture: design tokens, semantic naming (color, spacing, typography), and dark mode theming", "6.36.2 Headless UI pattern: separating component accessibility, keyboard navigation, and state logic from visual styling", "6.36.3 Radix UI / Floating UI primitives: anchor positioning, collision detection, portal rendering, and focus containment", "6.36.4 Tailwind CSS integration vs CSS Modules: class variance authority (cva), clsx, and tailwind-merge utility patterns", "6.36.5 Component polymorphism: the `asChild` pattern and polymorphic `as` prop implementations in TypeScript", "6.36.6 Building compound components: Select, DropdownMenu, Tabs, and Accordion with context communication"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Class name conflicts overriding critical design tokens; headless portal components breaking keyboard navigation order.", "How does Component Design Systems & Headless UI Architecture scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-6-37', 'phase-06-lesson-37-advanced-frontend-virtualization-infinite', 'module-8', 37, 'Lesson 8.37: Advanced Frontend Virtualization & Infinite Scrolling', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 6.1, Lesson 6.13', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 6.1, Lesson 6.13 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 220, 7, 1560.0, 1850.0, '# Lesson 6.37: Advanced Frontend Virtualization & Infinite Scrolling

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 6.1, Lesson 6.13
- **Subtopics**:
  - `6.37.1` DOM node explosion problem: memory consumption and layout calculation degradation with thousands of DOM nodes
  - `6.37.2` Virtualization architecture: viewport windowing, calculating visible row indices, and absolute item positioning
  - `6.37.3` TanStack Virtual (React Virtual) deep dive: fixed-size vs dynamic-size item height measurement
  - `6.37.4` Handling dynamic item heights: ResizeObserver, layout measuring, and scroll position correction
  - `6.37.5` Infinite scrolling integration: IntersectionObserver triggering batch page queries ahead of viewport bottom
  - `6.37.6` Scroll restoration: preserving exact scroll coordinates across client-side page transitions and browser back navigation
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Scroll jumping and jitter caused by uncalibrated dynamic item height changes; scrollbar thumb resizing erratically.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Build a virtualized data table rendering 100,000 live log rows smoothly at 60fps with variable row heights.
- **Project Application**: TenantIQ: Audit log viewer and real-time event streaming table.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 6 // Lesson 6.37: Advanced Frontend Virtualization & Infinite Scrolling\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Build a virtualized data table rendering 100,000 live log rows smoothly at 60fps with variable row h\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "6.37", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Build a virtualized data table rendering 100,000 live log rows smoothly at 60fps with variable row heights.", "subtopics": ["6.37.1 DOM node explosion problem: memory consumption and layout calculation degradation with thousands of DOM nodes", "6.37.2 Virtualization architecture: viewport windowing, calculating visible row indices, and absolute item positioning", "6.37.3 TanStack Virtual (React Virtual) deep dive: fixed-size vs dynamic-size item height measurement", "6.37.4 Handling dynamic item heights: ResizeObserver, layout measuring, and scroll position correction", "6.37.5 Infinite scrolling integration: IntersectionObserver triggering batch page queries ahead of viewport bottom", "6.37.6 Scroll restoration: preserving exact scroll coordinates across client-side page transitions and browser back navigation"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Scroll jumping and jitter caused by uncalibrated dynamic item height changes; scrollbar thumb resizing erratically.", "How does Advanced Frontend Virtualization & Infinite Scrolling scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-6-38', 'phase-06-lesson-38-real-time-frontend-communication-server-s', 'module-8', 38, 'Lesson 8.38: Real-Time Frontend Communication: Server-Sent Events (SSE) & WebSockets', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Lesson 6.14', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Lesson 6.14 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 220, 7, 1620.0, 1900.0, '# Lesson 6.38: Real-Time Frontend Communication: Server-Sent Events (SSE) & WebSockets

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 4, Lesson 6.14
- **Subtopics**:
  - `6.38.1` Real-time paradigms comparison: Polling vs Long Polling vs Server-Sent Events (SSE) vs WebSockets
  - `6.38.2` EventSource API: connection lifecycle, automatic reconnection, event types, and Last-Event-ID header
  - `6.38.3` ReadableStream fetch for SSE: overcoming HTTP/1.1 connection limits (6 per domain) using HTTP/2 multiplexed streams
  - `6.38.4` WebSocket client implementation: connection handshake, binary vs text frames, heartbeats (ping/pong), and backoff
  - `6.38.5` Reconnection resilience: managing network dropouts, offline queues, and state synchronization upon reconnect
  - `6.38.6` Integrating streaming data into React: dispatching real-time updates into state without causing frame-dropping re-renders
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Browser exhausting 6-connection HTTP/1.1 limit with raw EventSource; UI freezing from high-frequency WebSocket message flood.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Build a real-time collaborative activity feed consuming SSE streams with automatic reconnection and token refresh.
- **Project Application**: TenantIQ: Real-time tenant collaborative notification feed.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 6 // Lesson 6.38: Real-Time Frontend Communication: Server-Sent Events (SSE) & WebSockets\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Build a real-time collaborative activity feed consuming SSE streams with automatic reconnection and \n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "6.38", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Build a real-time collaborative activity feed consuming SSE streams with automatic reconnection and token refresh.", "subtopics": ["6.38.1 Real-time paradigms comparison: Polling vs Long Polling vs Server-Sent Events (SSE) vs WebSockets", "6.38.2 EventSource API: connection lifecycle, automatic reconnection, event types, and Last-Event-ID header", "6.38.3 ReadableStream fetch for SSE: overcoming HTTP/1.1 connection limits (6 per domain) using HTTP/2 multiplexed streams", "6.38.4 WebSocket client implementation: connection handshake, binary vs text frames, heartbeats (ping/pong), and backoff", "6.38.5 Reconnection resilience: managing network dropouts, offline queues, and state synchronization upon reconnect", "6.38.6 Integrating streaming data into React: dispatching real-time updates into state without causing frame-dropping re-renders"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Browser exhausting 6-connection HTTP/1.1 limit with raw EventSource; UI freezing from high-frequency WebSocket message f", "How does Real-Time Frontend Communication: Server-Sent Events (SSE) & WebSockets scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-6-39', 'phase-06-lesson-39-frontend-authentication-flows-oauth2-open', 'module-8', 39, 'Lesson 8.39: Frontend Authentication Flows: OAuth2, OpenID Connect & Session Storage', 'Prerequisites: Phase 5, Lesson 6.6', 'Prerequisites: Phase 5, Lesson 6.6 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 220, 7, 1500.0, 1950.0, '# Lesson 6.39: Frontend Authentication Flows: OAuth2, OpenID Connect & Session Storage

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 5, Lesson 6.6
- **Subtopics**:
  - `6.39.1` OAuth2 & OIDC on the client: Authorization Code Flow with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) mechanics
  - `6.39.2` Token storage debate: LocalStorage vs In-Memory + Refresh Cookie vs HttpOnly SameSite Session Cookies
  - `6.39.3` Silent token refreshing: proactive token rotation before expiration using background iframe or fetch calls
  - `6.39.4` Handling authentication state in React: auth providers, route protection, and preventing flash-of-unauthenticated-content
  - `6.39.5` Multi-factor authentication (MFA) UI flows: TOTP code entry, recovery codes, and step-up authentication
  - `6.39.6` Single Sign-On (SSO) integration: SAML/OIDC enterprise redirects and directory synchronization concepts
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Storing access tokens in LocalStorage exposing them to XSS exfiltration; race conditions during simultaneous token refresh calls.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement a secure PKCE client-side auth flow with HttpOnly refresh cookies and seamless automatic background token rotation.
- **Project Application**: TenantIQ: Multi-tenant auth pipeline supporting magic links and Google OAuth.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 6 // Lesson 6.39: Frontend Authentication Flows: OAuth2, OpenID Connect & Session Storage\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement a secure PKCE client-side auth flow with HttpOnly refresh cookies and seamless automatic b\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "6.39", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Implement a secure PKCE client-side auth flow with HttpOnly refresh cookies and seamless automatic background token rotation.", "subtopics": ["6.39.1 OAuth2 & OIDC on the client: Authorization Code Flow with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) mechanics", "6.39.2 Token storage debate: LocalStorage vs In-Memory + Refresh Cookie vs HttpOnly SameSite Session Cookies", "6.39.3 Silent token refreshing: proactive token rotation before expiration using background iframe or fetch calls", "6.39.4 Handling authentication state in React: auth providers, route protection, and preventing flash-of-unauthenticated-content", "6.39.5 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) UI flows: TOTP code entry, recovery codes, and step-up authentication", "6.39.6 Single Sign-On (SSO) integration: SAML/OIDC enterprise redirects and directory synchronization concepts"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Storing access tokens in LocalStorage exposing them to XSS exfiltration; race conditions during simultaneous token refre", "How does Frontend Authentication Flows: OAuth2, OpenID Connect & Session Storage scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-6-40', 'phase-06-lesson-40-payment-integration-stripe-elements-subsc', 'module-8', 40, 'Lesson 8.40: Payment Integration: Stripe Elements, Subscriptions & Checkout', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 6.34, Lesson 6.39', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 6.34, Lesson 6.39 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 220, 7, 1560.0, 2000.0, '# Lesson 6.40: Payment Integration: Stripe Elements, Subscriptions & Checkout

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 6.34, Lesson 6.39
- **Subtopics**:
  - `6.40.1` Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI-DSS) client-side compliance: why raw card details never touch servers
  - `6.40.2` Stripe Elements architecture: sandboxed iframes, PaymentElement, and unified payment methods
  - `6.40.3` Subscription billing lifecycles: products, prices, billing intervals (monthly/annual), and trial periods
  - `6.40.4` Multi-tenant SaaS billing models: per-seat pricing, metered usage-based billing, and plan tiers
  - `6.40.5` Handling payment failures: 3D Secure authentication popups, SCA compliance, and invoice past_due recovery flows
  - `6.40.6` Customer Portal integration: delegating payment method updates, invoice downloads, and subscription cancellations to Stripe
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Failing to handle 3D Secure modal challenges causing payment abandonment; webhook processing races during checkout completion.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement a multi-tier SaaS billing upgrade flow with Stripe PaymentElement, subscription management, and webhook reconciliation.
- **Project Application**: TenantIQ: Billing checkout and subscription tier upgrade pipeline.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 6 // Lesson 6.40: Payment Integration: Stripe Elements, Subscriptions & Checkout\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement a multi-tier SaaS billing upgrade flow with Stripe PaymentElement, subscription management\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "6.40", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Implement a multi-tier SaaS billing upgrade flow with Stripe PaymentElement, subscription management, and webhook reconciliation.", "subtopics": ["6.40.1 Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI-DSS) client-side compliance: why raw card details never touch servers", "6.40.2 Stripe Elements architecture: sandboxed iframes, PaymentElement, and unified payment methods", "6.40.3 Subscription billing lifecycles: products, prices, billing intervals (monthly/annual), and trial periods", "6.40.4 Multi-tenant SaaS billing models: per-seat pricing, metered usage-based billing, and plan tiers", "6.40.5 Handling payment failures: 3D Secure authentication popups, SCA compliance, and invoice past_due recovery flows", "6.40.6 Customer Portal integration: delegating payment method updates, invoice downloads, and subscription cancellations to Stripe"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Failing to handle 3D Secure modal challenges causing payment abandonment; webhook processing races during checkout compl", "How does Payment Integration: Stripe Elements, Subscriptions & Checkout scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-8-1', 'phase-08-lesson-01-the-4-step-system-design-interview-framew', 'module-9', 1, 'Lesson 9.1: The 4-Step System Design Interview Framework & Requirement Scoping', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Phase 7', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Phase 7 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 260, 9, 2060.0, 50.0, '# Lesson 8.1: The 4-Step System Design Interview Framework & Requirement Scoping

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 4, Phase 7
- **Subtopics**:
  - `8.1.1` Step 1: Scoping functional requirements (core user journeys) vs non-functional requirements (latency, availability, scale)
  - `8.1.2` Defining out-of-scope boundaries to prevent architectural derailment during interview discussions
  - `8.1.3` Step 2: Back-of-the-envelope capacity estimation: QPS, bandwidth, storage, memory, and cache projections
  - `8.1.4` Step 3: High-Level Architecture (HLA): client, CDN, load balancer, API gateway, stateless services, cache, and database
  - `8.1.5` Step 4: Deep Dive & Bottlenecks: identifying single points of failure, partition tolerance, and failover workflows
  - `8.1.6` Collaborative communication: driving the discussion, active listening, asking clarifying questions, and managing time
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Jumping straight into database schemas or microservices without scoping functional and non-functional requirements.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Write a structured 1-page system requirements specification (SRS) for a global link shortening platform under 15 minutes.
- **Project Application**: System Design Portfolio: Requirement scoping templates.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 8 // Lesson 8.1: The 4-Step System Design Interview Framework & Requirement Scoping\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Write a structured 1-page system requirements specification (SRS) for a global link shortening platf\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "8.1", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Write a structured 1-page system requirements specification (SRS) for a global link shortening platform under 15 minutes.", "subtopics": ["8.1.1 Step 1: Scoping functional requirements (core user journeys) vs non-functional requirements (latency, availability, scale)", "8.1.2 Defining out-of-scope boundaries to prevent architectural derailment during interview discussions", "8.1.3 Step 2: Back-of-the-envelope capacity estimation: QPS, bandwidth, storage, memory, and cache projections", "8.1.4 Step 3: High-Level Architecture (HLA): client, CDN, load balancer, API gateway, stateless services, cache, and database", "8.1.5 Step 4: Deep Dive & Bottlenecks: identifying single points of failure, partition tolerance, and failover workflows", "8.1.6 Collaborative communication: driving the discussion, active listening, asking clarifying questions, and managing time"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Jumping straight into database schemas or microservices without scoping functional and non-functional requirements.", "How does The 4-Step System Design Interview Framework & Requirement Scoping scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-8-2', 'phase-08-lesson-02-back-of-the-envelope-estimation-numbers-e', 'module-9', 2, 'Lesson 9.2: Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation: Numbers Every Engineer Must Know', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0, Lesson 8.1', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0, Lesson 8.1 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 260, 9, 2120.0, 100.0, '# Lesson 8.2: Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation: Numbers Every Engineer Must Know

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 0, Lesson 8.1
- **Subtopics**:
  - `8.2.1` Latency numbers to memorize: L1 cache (1ns), RAM (100ns), SSD read (100µs), HDD seek (10ms), cross-datacenter RTT (150ms)
  - `8.2.2` Throughput calculations: converting Monthly Active Users (MAU) and Daily Active Users (DAU) to Read/Write QPS
  - `8.2.3` Peak multiplier heuristics: average QPS vs peak QPS (typically $2\times$ to $5\times$ average load)
  - `8.2.4` Storage volumetric estimation: bytes per record $\times$ records per day $\times$ retention years $\times$ replication factor
  - `8.2.5` Bandwidth volumetric estimation: ingress bandwidth (write QPS $\times$ payload size) vs egress bandwidth (read QPS $\times$ payload size)
  - `8.2.6` Memory and cache sizing: 80/20 Pareto rule (20% of hot data generates 80% of read traffic) memory provisioning
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Confusing bits (Gbps) with bytes (GB/s); failing to account for peak load spikes leading to 50% infrastructure under-provisioning.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Calculate exact QPS, storage (5-year), RAM cache, and bandwidth requirements for Twitter (500M DAU) with zero calculator aids.
- **Project Application**: System Design Portfolio: Automated capacity planning spreadsheet.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 8 // Lesson 8.2: Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation: Numbers Every Engineer Must Know\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Calculate exact QPS, storage (5-year), RAM cache, and bandwidth requirements for Twitter (500M DAU) \n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "8.2", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Calculate exact QPS, storage (5-year), RAM cache, and bandwidth requirements for Twitter (500M DAU) with zero calculator aids.", "subtopics": ["8.2.1 Latency numbers to memorize: L1 cache (1ns), RAM (100ns), SSD read (100\u00b5s), HDD seek (10ms), cross-datacenter RTT (150ms)", "8.2.2 Throughput calculations: converting Monthly Active Users (MAU) and Daily Active Users (DAU) to Read/Write QPS", "8.2.3 Peak multiplier heuristics: average QPS vs peak QPS (typically $2\\times$ to $5\\times$ average load)", "8.2.4 Storage volumetric estimation: bytes per record $\\times$ records per day $\\times$ retention years $\\times$ replication factor", "8.2.5 Bandwidth volumetric estimation: ingress bandwidth (write QPS $\\times$ payload size) vs egress bandwidth (read QPS $\\times$ payload size)", "8.2.6 Memory and cache sizing: 80/20 Pareto rule (20% of hot data generates 80% of read traffic) memory provisioning"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Confusing bits (Gbps) with bytes (GB/s); failing to account for peak load spikes leading to 50% infrastructure under-pro", "How does Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation: Numbers Every Engineer Must Know scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-8-3', 'phase-08-lesson-03-data-storage-paradigms-database-selection', 'module-9', 3, 'Lesson 9.3: Data Storage Paradigms & Database Selection Matrix', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Phase 7', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Phase 7 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 260, 9, 2000.0, 150.0, '# Lesson 8.3: Data Storage Paradigms & Database Selection Matrix

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 4, Phase 7
- **Subtopics**:
  - `8.3.1` Relational (RDBMS) vs NoSQL: ACID guarantees vs horizontal scalability, schema flexibility, and query patterns
  - `8.3.2` Document stores (MongoDB): nested denormalized hierarchical documents, index structures, and write-heavy workloads
  - `8.3.3` Wide-column stores (Cassandra, ScyllaDB): log-structured merge trees (LSM), tunable consistency, high write throughput
  - `8.3.4` Key-Value stores (Redis, DynamoDB): lightning sub-millisecond lookups, TTL expirations, and partition keys
  - `8.3.5` Graph databases (Neo4j): vertex and edge traversal, index-free adjacency, and social graph fraud detection
  - `8.3.6` Time-series databases (InfluxDB, TimescaleDB): columnar compression, downsampling, and high-frequency sensor/metric ingestion
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Choosing NoSQL for financial balances without transactional multi-record atomicity; choosing RDBMS for petabyte append-only logs.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Draft a comprehensive technical decision matrix comparing PostgreSQL, Cassandra, DynamoDB, and Redis across 8 operational criteria.
- **Project Application**: System Design Portfolio: Database architectural selection guide.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 8 // Lesson 8.3: Data Storage Paradigms & Database Selection Matrix\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Draft a comprehensive technical decision matrix comparing PostgreSQL, Cassandra, DynamoDB, and Redis\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "8.3", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Draft a comprehensive technical decision matrix comparing PostgreSQL, Cassandra, DynamoDB, and Redis across 8 operational criteria.", "subtopics": ["8.3.1 Relational (RDBMS) vs NoSQL: ACID guarantees vs horizontal scalability, schema flexibility, and query patterns", "8.3.2 Document stores (MongoDB): nested denormalized hierarchical documents, index structures, and write-heavy workloads", "8.3.3 Wide-column stores (Cassandra, ScyllaDB): log-structured merge trees (LSM), tunable consistency, high write throughput", "8.3.4 Key-Value stores (Redis, DynamoDB): lightning sub-millisecond lookups, TTL expirations, and partition keys", "8.3.5 Graph databases (Neo4j): vertex and edge traversal, index-free adjacency, and social graph fraud detection", "8.3.6 Time-series databases (InfluxDB, TimescaleDB): columnar compression, downsampling, and high-frequency sensor/metric ingestion"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Choosing NoSQL for financial balances without transactional multi-record atomicity; choosing RDBMS for petabyte append-o", "How does Data Storage Paradigms & Database Selection Matrix scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-8-4', 'phase-08-lesson-04-caching-architectures-invalidation-thunde', 'module-9', 4, 'Lesson 9.4: Caching Architectures: Invalidation, Thundering Herd & Topologies', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Lesson 8.2', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Lesson 8.2 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 260, 9, 2060.0, 200.0, '# Lesson 8.4: Caching Architectures: Invalidation, Thundering Herd & Topologies

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 4, Lesson 8.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `8.4.1` Caching topologies: In-memory local cache vs Distributed cache clusters (Redis, Memcached) vs Multi-tier caching
  - `8.4.2` Cache access patterns: Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading), Read-Through, Write-Through, and Write-Behind (Write-Back)
  - `8.4.3` Cache eviction policies: Least Recently Used (LRU), Least Frequently Used (LFU), First In First Out (FIFO)
  - `8.4.4` Cache Invalidation problems: cache stampede / thundering herd mitigation using mutex locking, probabilistic early expiration (XFetch)
  - `8.4.5` Cache Penetration (queries for non-existent keys): mitigating via null caching and Bloom filters
  - `8.4.6` Cache Breakdown (hot key expiry) and Cache Avalanche (simultaneous massive key expirations) mitigation with TTL jitter
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Cache avalanche crashing core databases when 100,000 keys expire at midnight simultaneously without randomized TTL jitter.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement the XFetch probabilistic early cache expiration algorithm in Python; simulate high-concurrency read traffic under load.
- **Project Application**: System Design Portfolio: High-throughput caching architectural patterns.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 8 // Lesson 8.4: Caching Architectures: Invalidation, Thundering Herd & Topologies\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement the XFetch probabilistic early cache expiration algorithm in Python; simulate high-concurr\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "8.4", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Implement the XFetch probabilistic early cache expiration algorithm in Python; simulate high-concurrency read traffic under load.", "subtopics": ["8.4.1 Caching topologies: In-memory local cache vs Distributed cache clusters (Redis, Memcached) vs Multi-tier caching", "8.4.2 Cache access patterns: Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading), Read-Through, Write-Through, and Write-Behind (Write-Back)", "8.4.3 Cache eviction policies: Least Recently Used (LRU), Least Frequently Used (LFU), First In First Out (FIFO)", "8.4.4 Cache Invalidation problems: cache stampede / thundering herd mitigation using mutex locking, probabilistic early expiration (XFetch)", "8.4.5 Cache Penetration (queries for non-existent keys): mitigating via null caching and Bloom filters", "8.4.6 Cache Breakdown (hot key expiry) and Cache Avalanche (simultaneous massive key expirations) mitigation with TTL jitter"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Cache avalanche crashing core databases when 100,000 keys expire at midnight simultaneously without randomized TTL jitte", "How does Caching Architectures: Invalidation, Thundering Herd & Topologies scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
